    "result_text": "The build succeeds and the test passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved the date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July). The change is centered on one new small, cohesive helper so the existing methods barely grew.\n\n**New unit \u2014 `model/FiscalYear.java`:** three trivial static methods over the business-day-adjusted registration date:\n- `startYearOf(date)` \u2014 the calendar year the containing fiscal year starts in (month \u2265 July \u2192 that year, else previous year).\n- `labelOf(date)` \u2014 `FY<YY>`.\n- `elapsedSince(date)` \u2014 whole fiscal years from the date to today.\n\n**Wiring (one edit each):**\n- `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 added a `fiscalYear` \u2192 `FiscalYear.labelOf(...)` mapping, switched `membershipNumber`'s `YY` segment from the calendar year to `FiscalYear.startYearOf(...)`, and registered `FiscalYear` in the mapper's imports.\n- `model/MembershipLevels.pointsOf` \u2014 the `+3` tenure rule now triggers on `FiscalYear.elapsedSince(...) > 1` instead of a 365-day window.\n- `resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated DTO gets the field) and corrected the `membershipNumber`/`membershipPoints` descriptions.\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** all real logic lives in the new class where surrounding complexity (`WMC_other`) is near zero; each pre-existing method I touched stayed simple and changed only a line or two. The `pointsOf` edit actually reduced its branching, and the mapper edits are single annotation lines.\n\nThe behaviour changes exactly as specified: `fiscalYear` is returned as `FY<YY>`, the membership identifier's year segment uses the fiscal year, and tenure is counted in elapsed fiscal years. `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec fixed the format (`FY<YY>`) but not whether the year label is the fiscal year's *start* or *end* year, nor the exact tenure threshold. I labelled by the start year (so a Sept-2026 date \u2192 `FY26`, consistent with the pre-existing `M26` example) and kept the tenure test as strictly greater than one elapsed fiscal year to preserve the old \"over one year\" spirit. No test pins these, but if your convention labels by the ending year, that's a one-line change in `FiscalYear`.",
